// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["iii-c"] = {"qs": [["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "A sovereign wealth fund and an unregulated family office both apply for FPI registration. Which is the correct classification?", ["Sovereign wealth fund as Category II; family office as Category I", "Both as Category II", "Both as Category I", "Sovereign wealth fund as Category I; family office as Category II"], 3, "Government and government-related investors such as sovereign wealth funds fall in Category I. Unregulated family offices and individuals fall in Category II, which carries different KYC requirements."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "A recognised stock exchange delists the securities of a company. Where can the company appeal against this decision?", ["Reserve Bank of India", "National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission", "Securities Appellate Tribunal", "Registrar of Companies"], 2, "Section 21A of the SCRA permits a listed company or an aggrieved investor to appeal to SAT against a delisting decision of a recognised stock exchange. Consumer forums and the ROC have no role in delisting appeals."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "Under Section 15HA of the SEBI Act, the penalty for fraudulent and unfair trade practices can extend to:", ["₹1 lakh or the amount of profit made by the person, whichever is lower", "₹25 crore or three times the profits made, whichever is higher", "₹10 lakh only, irrespective of profit made", "Twice the brokerage earned on the trades"], 1, "Section 15HA provides a penalty of not less than ₹5 lakh, which may extend to ₹25 crore or three times the profits made, whichever is higher. The fixed lower amounts in the other options are not correct."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "A broker uses securities lying in Client A's account to meet the pay-in obligation of Client B without authorisation. Under PFUTP, this is:", ["Misuse of client securities, a prohibited fraudulent practice", "An approved securities lending transaction", "Permitted if Client B repays within a week", "A normal netting practice allowed for all brokers under exchange bye-laws"], 0, "Using one client's funds or securities for another client or for the broker's own purpose without authority is a fraudulent practice. Securities lending is permitted only through the approved SLB mechanism with consent."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "Which of the following is NOT a market infrastructure institution (MII) in the Indian securities market?", ["A recognised clearing corporation", "An asset management company", "A depository", "A recognised stock exchange"], 1, "Stock exchanges, clearing corporations and depositories are MIIs because they provide the core trading, settlement and holding infrastructure. An asset management company is a market intermediary that manages mutual fund schemes, not an MII."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "SEBI wants to direct a mutual fund to refund money to investors to protect their interests. Under which provision does SEBI generally issue such directions?", ["Section 12 of the SEBI Act", "Section 15Z of the SEBI Act", "Section 26 of the SEBI Act", "Section 11B of the SEBI Act"], 3, "Section 11B empowers SEBI to issue directions to any person or intermediary in the interest of investors or orderly development of the market. Section 12 deals with registration and Section 15Z with appeals to the Supreme Court."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "An AMC's compliance team checks a sample of completed transactions each quarter against internal policies and regulatory limits. This activity is called:", ["Valuation of the scheme's assets", "Credit rating of the scheme portfolio", "Statutory audit of financial statements", "Compliance testing and monitoring"], 3, "Periodic sample checks against rules are compliance monitoring and testing, which help confirm that controls work. A statutory audit focuses on financial statements, while rating and valuation are different processes."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "What is the main purpose of setting up KYC Registration Agencies in the securities market?", ["To collect brokerage and other charges on behalf of all stock brokers", "To let a client complete KYC once and use it across intermediaries", "To issue PAN cards to new investors", "To settle trades between clearing members"], 1, "KRAs centralise KYC records so that an investor who has completed KYC with one SEBI intermediary need not repeat the full process with others. Issuing PAN or settling trades is not their function."], ["SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations", "Under the MF Regulations, a scheme may be wound up if unitholders pass a resolution to that effect. What proportion of unitholders is required?", ["51% of the unitholders", "75% of the unitholders", "90% of the unitholders", "66% of the unitholders"], 1, "A close-ended or open-ended scheme can be wound up if 75% of its unitholders pass a resolution to that effect, among other grounds such as the trustees' opinion or a SEBI direction."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "Which of the following is NOT a 'security' as defined under the SCRA, 1956?", ["Units issued by a mutual fund scheme to investors", "A government security", "An equity derivative contract", "A fixed deposit placed with a commercial bank"], 3, "The SCRA definition includes shares, bonds, derivatives, units of mutual funds and collective investment schemes, government securities and similar instruments. A bank fixed deposit is a deposit, not a marketable security."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "A person is treated as a 'connected person' if, in the company's context, he was associated with it during what period before the concerned act?", ["Three years", "One year", "Six months", "One month"], 2, "The PIT Regulations treat a person as connected if, during the six months prior to the concerned act, he was associated with the company in a way that gives or may reasonably be expected to give access to UPSI."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "A large company sells non-convertible debentures directly to investors through a public issue instead of taking a bank loan. This is an example of:", ["Direct (market-based) finance", "Financial repression by the regulator", "Money market borrowing by the issuer", "Indirect finance through a bank"], 0, "When the borrower raises funds straight from investors by issuing securities, it is direct or market-based finance. A bank loan would be indirect finance because the bank stands between saver and borrower."], ["SEBI (REIT) Regulations", "Which party is responsible for managing a REIT's assets and investments and for day-to-day operations?", ["The manager", "The auditor", "The valuer", "The trustee"], 0, "The manager of a REIT makes investment decisions and manages the real estate assets, either directly or through property managers. The trustee oversees on behalf of unitholders, and the valuer only values assets."], ["SEBI (InvIT) Regulations", "Units of an InvIT offered to the public are required to be:", ["Transferred only with the trustee's prior approval", "Held only in physical form by unitholders", "Listed on a recognised stock exchange", "Redeemable on demand at NAV like an open-ended fund"], 2, "Units of publicly offered InvITs must be listed on a recognised stock exchange so investors can trade them. InvIT units are not redeemable on demand like open-ended mutual fund units."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "An intermediary wishes to give up its registration voluntarily. What should it ensure before SEBI accepts the surrender?", ["Pending client obligations and complaints are settled or suitably provided for", "Its employees are transferred to another SEBI intermediary", "Its shares are listed on a recognised stock exchange first", "Its competitors and industry associations are informed about the surrender in writing"], 0, "SEBI accepts surrender after it is satisfied that client interests are protected, for example that pending obligations, assets and complaints are dealt with. Informing competitors or listing shares has no bearing on surrender."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "An AMC has filed an STR on a client. The client calls the AMC asking whether any report has been made about him. What should the staff do?", ["Withdraw the STR if the client gives an explanation", "Confirm the report so the client can explain the transactions", "Not disclose the STR, as tipping off the client is prohibited", "Send the client a copy of the STR for his records"], 2, "Reporting entities and their staff must keep STRs strictly confidential and must not tip off the client. The STR, once filed, is not withdrawn because the client offers an explanation."], ["SEBI (InvIT) Regulations", "In an InvIT, which party is responsible for executing and operating the infrastructure project as per the agreement?", ["The project manager", "The valuer", "The trustee", "The unitholders"], 0, "The project manager executes and operates the infrastructure projects or achieves their completion as per the project implementation agreement. The trustee oversees the InvIT for unitholders, and the investment manager handles investment decisions."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "One member of the SEBI board is nominated from which of the following institutions?", ["Reserve Bank of India", "Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India", "Comptroller and Auditor General of India", "National Stock Exchange"], 0, "The SEBI board includes a Chairman, two members from the Central Government ministries, one member from RBI and five other members. No member is nominated from IRDAI, a stock exchange or the CAG."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "FIU-IND, the central agency for receiving and analysing financial intelligence, functions under:", ["Securities and Exchange Board of India", "Ministry of Home Affairs", "Reserve Bank of India", "Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance"], 3, "The Financial Intelligence Unit – India is an independent body reporting to the Economic Intelligence Council and functions under the Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance. It is not part of SEBI or RBI."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "Which of the following is the most important benefit that an active secondary market gives to investors?", ["Protection from all market price risk", "Fresh capital for the issuing company", "A guaranteed minimum return on every investment made", "Liquidity and continuous price discovery"], 3, "A secondary market lets investors exit by selling to other investors and reveals fair prices through trading. It does not raise new capital for the issuer, nor does it guarantee returns or remove price risk."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "Under the SCRR, 1957, a listed company must generally maintain public shareholding of at least:", ["25%", "51%", "35%", "10%"], 0, "Rule 19A of the SCRR requires every listed company (other than certain exempted public sector or specified cases) to maintain minimum public shareholding of 25%. If it falls below, the company must restore it within the specified time."], ["Foreign Exchange Management Act", "Which agency investigates contraventions of FEMA?", ["FIU-IND", "Serious Fraud Investigation Office", "Central Bureau of Investigation", "Directorate of Enforcement"], 3, "The Directorate of Enforcement is empowered to investigate contraventions under FEMA and also enforces the PMLA. SFIO deals with corporate frauds under the Companies Act, and FIU-IND collects and analyses financial intelligence."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "Why should the compliance function of a fund intermediary be independent of its business teams?", ["So that compliance staff can earn incentives linked to sales volumes", "So that compliance does not need to report to the board at all", "So it can assess and report breaches objectively without pressure from revenue targets", "So that business heads can review and approve all compliance reports before they are submitted"], 2, "Independence lets compliance staff challenge business decisions and escalate breaches without conflict. Linking compliance pay to sales or routing reports through business heads would undermine that objectivity."], ["SEBI (InvIT) Regulations", "The trustee of an InvIT is primarily responsible for:", ["Overseeing that the InvIT and its manager act in unitholders' interest", "Providing the bulk of the InvIT's borrowing", "Operating and maintaining the toll roads or power lines", "Setting the daily trading price of the InvIT's units on the stock exchange"], 0, "The trustee holds the InvIT assets in trust for unitholders and oversees the activities of the investment manager and others to ensure compliance and protect unitholders. Operations are with the project manager."], ["SEBI (InvIT) Regulations", "Which entity in an InvIT structure makes investment decisions and oversees the activities of the project manager?", ["The stock exchange", "The sponsor", "The registrar to the issue", "The investment manager"], 3, "The investment manager takes investment decisions for the InvIT and is responsible for overseeing the project manager's performance. The sponsor sets up the InvIT, but day-to-day management sits with the investment manager."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "Two parties enter into a derivative contract on a stock outside any recognised exchange and without any clearing arrangement. Under Section 18A of the SCRA, derivative contracts are legal and valid when they are:", ["Approved individually by the Central Government in advance", "Traded on a recognised exchange and settled through its clearing house or corporation", "Registered with the Registrar of Companies within 30 days", "Signed by both parties on stamp paper before a notary public"], 1, "Section 18A makes derivative contracts legal and valid if they are traded on a recognised stock exchange and settled on its clearing house or a recognised clearing corporation, as per its rules and bye-laws. Notarisation or ROC filing does not validate them."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "Under the Code of Practices and Procedures for Fair Disclosure, which official deals with universal dissemination and disclosure of UPSI?", ["Chief Investor Relations Officer", "Chief Risk Officer", "Registrar and transfer agent", "Company's statutory auditor"], 0, "Listed companies must designate a senior officer as Chief Investor Relations Officer to deal with dissemination of information and disclosure of UPSI. The RTA and auditor have no role in fair disclosure of UPSI."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "A group of connected entities repeatedly buy and sell the same shares among themselves to create an impression of heavy trading interest. This is:", ["A legitimate block deal executed through the separate trading window", "Hedging of an existing portfolio position", "Circular trading to create a false appearance of volume", "Arbitrage between two stock exchanges"], 2, "Trades among a closed group that create artificial volumes or prices are circular trades, a manipulative practice. Arbitrage and hedging are legitimate strategies, and block deals follow a separate disclosed mechanism."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "Under the KRA framework, what happens when key attributes of a client's KYC record (such as PAN, address, mobile number or email) cannot be validated?", ["The intermediary is fined automatically by the stock exchange for the error", "The record is permanently deleted without any notice", "The client's securities are automatically sold by the KRA", "The record may be placed 'on hold' until the attributes are verified"], 3, "KRAs now validate key KYC attributes against official sources. Records that fail validation may be marked 'on hold', and the client must update or re-verify details before transacting. No automatic sale or deletion takes place."], ["SEBI (AIF) Regulations", "A Special Situation Fund that invests in stressed loans and other special situation assets falls in which AIF category?", ["It is not an AIF", "Category III", "Category II", "Category I"], 3, "SEBI introduced Special Situation Funds in 2022 as a sub-category of Category I AIFs to invest in stressed assets, including loans acquired under the relevant frameworks."], ["SEBI (InvIT) Regulations", "Where an InvIT invests in an SPV through a holding company, the InvIT (directly or through the holdco) must generally hold at least what share of the SPV's equity?", ["100%", "26%", "51%", "40%"], 2, "The InvIT Regulations require the InvIT, directly or through a holdco, to hold a controlling interest and at least 51% of the equity share capital or interest in the SPV, except where restricted by law or concession terms."], ["SEBI (REIT) Regulations", "Unitholders holding at least what percentage of a REIT's units have the right to nominate one director on the board of the manager?", ["1%", "10%", "26%", "51%"], 1, "SEBI's 2023 amendment gave eligible unitholders holding 10% or more of the total outstanding units the right to nominate one non-independent director on the manager's board, improving governance."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "Under Chapter VI-A of the SEBI Act, monetary penalties for specified defaults are imposed by:", ["The Securities Appellate Tribunal on its own", "The Central Government through a notification", "An adjudicating officer appointed by SEBI", "The stock exchange where the default occurred"], 2, "SEBI appoints adjudicating officers to hold inquiries and impose penalties under Chapter VI-A. SAT is an appellate body, and stock exchanges act under their own bye-laws, not Chapter VI-A."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Under the PMLA, records of transactions and client identification must generally be maintained for at least:", ["Three years", "Ten years", "Five years", "One year"], 2, "Section 12 of the PMLA requires reporting entities to maintain transaction records and identity records for five years from the date of transaction or end of the business relationship, as applicable. Longer retention may apply if investigations are pending."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "What does 'demutualisation' of a stock exchange under the SCRA mean?", ["Merger of two or more stock exchanges into one", "Conversion of the exchange into a department of the Ministry of Finance", "Closure of the exchange and transfer of its members", "Segregation of ownership and management from trading rights of members"], 3, "Demutualisation separates ownership and management of the exchange from the trading rights of its members, reducing conflicts of interest. Under a mutual structure, broker-members owned and ran the exchange."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "The National Pension System (NPS) and pension fund managers are regulated by:", ["RBI", "IRDAI", "PFRDA", "Ministry of Corporate Affairs"], 2, "The Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority (PFRDA) administers the NPS and registers pension fund managers. IRDAI covers insurance and RBI covers banking, so they are not the primary regulator for the NPS."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "An investor suffers a loss because of negligence by his depository participant. Under the Depositories Act, who must indemnify him?", ["The depository, which may then recover from the participant", "SEBI out of its own funds", "The issuer company whose shares were affected", "The stock exchange where the shares are listed"], 0, "Section 16 makes the depository liable to indemnify the beneficial owner for losses caused by negligence of the depository or its participant. The depository can in turn seek recovery from the participant at fault."], ["SEBI (REIT) Regulations", "A REIT plans to buy a property from its sponsor. How should this related party transaction be handled?", ["At arm's length, with valuation and disclosure, and unitholder approval where required", "Only with the oral consent of the sponsor's chairman", "Only after delisting the REIT units from the exchange and seeking approval of the sponsor's lenders", "Without any valuation since the sponsor is trusted"], 0, "Related party transactions must be on an arm's length basis, in the unitholders' best interest, consistent with REIT strategy, supported by valuation reports and disclosed, with unitholder approval above specified thresholds."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "A registered intermediary facing SEBI proceedings wants to resolve the matter without admitting or denying the findings. Which route can it use?", ["Request the stock exchange to withdraw the case", "Surrender its registration to end the proceedings", "File a writ petition before the Securities Appellate Tribunal immediately", "Apply for settlement under the SEBI settlement proceedings framework"], 3, "SEBI's settlement framework allows a noticee to settle administrative and civil proceedings by paying a settlement amount, without admission or denial of guilt. Surrendering registration does not automatically end proceedings already initiated."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "A designated person buys shares of his company. Under the code of conduct, he generally cannot sell them (enter a contra trade) within the next:", ["One month", "Six months", "One year", "Three months"], 1, "Designated persons must not execute a contra trade within six months of a trade. If one is executed in violation, the profits are liable to be disgorged and remitted to the Investor Protection and Education Fund."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "A director of a listed company trades its shares worth a total of ₹12 lakh during a calendar quarter. Under continual disclosure, he must inform the company because the value exceeds:", ["₹10 lakh in a calendar quarter", "₹5 lakh in a single day", "₹1 crore in a calendar quarter", "₹25 lakh in a financial year"], 0, "Promoters, directors, KMP and designated persons must disclose trades whose aggregate value exceeds ₹10 lakh in a calendar quarter within two trading days. The company then informs the stock exchanges within two trading days."], ["SEBI (InvIT) Regulations", "Under the InvIT Regulations, an InvIT is set up as:", ["A private limited company incorporated under the Companies Act, 2013", "A trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act, 1882 and with SEBI", "A limited liability partnership under the LLP Act", "A cooperative society under State law"], 1, "An InvIT is created as a trust under the Indian Trusts Act, 1882, with a registered trust deed, and must be registered with SEBI. It is not a company, LLP or cooperative."], ["Foreign Exchange Management Act", "A bank authorised by RBI to deal in foreign exchange is known under FEMA as an:", ["Approved intermediary", "Authorised person (authorised dealer)", "Designated depository participant", "Accredited investor"], 1, "Under Section 10 of FEMA, RBI can authorise persons such as banks to deal in foreign exchange; banks so authorised are authorised dealers. DDPs and accredited investors are SEBI concepts, not FEMA terms."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "Ultimate responsibility for ensuring that an asset management company complies with regulations rests with:", ["The registrar and transfer agent", "The mutual fund distributors empanelled by the AMC", "Its board of directors and senior management", "Only the compliance officer personally"], 2, "The compliance officer monitors and advises, but accountability for compliance lies with the board and senior management, who set the 'tone at the top'. Distributors and RTAs have their own obligations but do not bear the AMC's overall responsibility."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "A distributor sells a high-risk credit risk debt scheme to an 80-year-old retired investor seeking capital safety, without checking suitability or explaining risks. Under PFUTP, this is an example of:", ["Legitimate portfolio rebalancing", "A permitted execution-only transaction by the distributor", "Mis-selling of units of a mutual fund scheme", "Market making in mutual fund units"], 2, "PFUTP treats mis-selling to include selling without reasonable regard to the investor's suitability and without disclosing risk factors. Ignoring the investor's risk profile and needs amounts to mis-selling."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "In the depository system, a depository participant (DP) acts as:", ["The issuer of the securities held by investors", "An agent of the depository providing services to investors", "A regulator of the depository on behalf of SEBI", "The registered owner of all the securities held in the depository system"], 1, "A DP is an agent of the depository through which investors open demat accounts and access depository services. The depository is the registered owner, and SEBI, not the DP, regulates depositories."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "Offshore derivative instruments (such as participatory notes) with Indian securities as underlying can be issued only by:", ["Indian stock brokers to foreign clients", "Indian mutual funds through their overseas offices", "Category I FPIs, subject to SEBI conditions", "Any Category II FPI without restriction"], 2, "ODIs can be issued only by Category I FPIs (other than certain excluded types), subject to KYC, reporting and other conditions. Category II FPIs, Indian brokers and mutual funds are not allowed to issue them."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "A company plans to share UPSI with a merchant banker for due diligence on a proposed acquisition. Under the regulations, this sharing is permitted only if it is:", ["Approved verbally by any director of the company", "For legitimate purposes, performance of duties or discharge of legal obligations", "Communicated only through a private messaging app", "Paid for by the merchant banker at a fair price"], 1, "UPSI may be communicated only in furtherance of legitimate purposes, performance of duties or discharge of legal obligations, and recipients must be bound by confidentiality. Approval by any director or the mode of communication does not make it lawful."], ["SEBI (AIF) Regulations", "What is the minimum corpus required for each scheme of an AIF (other than an angel fund)?", ["₹20 crore", "₹100 crore", "₹5 crore", "₹50 crore"], 0, "Each AIF scheme, other than an angel fund, must have a corpus of at least ₹20 crore. This is different from the minimum investment per investor, which is generally ₹1 crore."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "A dealer at a broking firm learns of a large buy order from an institutional client and buys the same stock in his own account just before placing the client's order. This practice is called:", ["Front running", "Bear raiding", "Insider trading", "Circular trading"], 0, "Trading ahead of a substantial client order using knowledge of that order is front running, specifically prohibited under PFUTP. Insider trading involves UPSI about the company, which is a different offence."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Under the PML Rules, the person designated by a reporting entity to ensure overall compliance with PMLA obligations, usually a whole-time director, is the:", ["Nodal Investor Officer", "Designated Director", "Compliance Auditor", "Principal Officer"], 1, "The Designated Director ensures overall compliance with PMLA obligations and is typically the managing or a whole-time director. The Principal Officer is responsible for furnishing information and reports to FIU-IND."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "What is the core economic function of a financial system?", ["Collecting direct and indirect taxes for the government", "Printing currency notes as required by businesses", "Channelling savings from surplus units to deficit units", "Fixing the prices of goods and services in the economy"], 2, "A financial system mobilises savings from those with surplus funds and allocates them to those who need funds for investment or consumption. Price-setting, taxation and currency issue are not its core function."], ["SEBI (REIT) Regulations", "Under the REIT Regulations, a REIT may invest in real estate assets located:", ["Anywhere in the world without restriction", "Only in Special Economic Zones abroad", "Only in India", "In India and any FATF member country"], 2, "REITs registered with SEBI can invest only in real estate and related assets located in India. Overseas property investment is not permitted under the regulations."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "An insurance company is aggrieved by an order passed by IRDAI. Where does its appeal lie?", ["Securities Appellate Tribunal", "Debt Recovery Tribunal", "National Company Law Tribunal", "Directly to the Supreme Court"], 0, "The Securities Appellate Tribunal hears appeals against orders of SEBI, and its jurisdiction has been extended to orders of IRDAI and PFRDA. NCLT handles company law and insolvency matters, not insurance regulatory orders."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "An investor wishes to convert securities held in his demat account back into physical certificates. This process is called:", ["Transmission", "Dematerialisation", "Rematerialisation", "Transposition"], 2, "Converting electronic holdings back to physical form is rematerialisation, the opposite of dematerialisation. Transmission is transfer on death, and transposition is a change in the order of joint holders' names."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "In the money laundering process, moving illegal funds through a series of complex transactions to hide their source is known as:", ["Placement", "Structuring", "Integration", "Layering"], 3, "Placement introduces illicit cash into the financial system, layering obscures its trail through multiple transactions, and integration brings it back as apparently legitimate money. Structuring is breaking amounts to avoid reporting thresholds."], ["Foreign Exchange Management Act", "Under FEMA, a person is generally a 'person resident in India' if he has resided in India for more than how many days in the preceding financial year?", ["365 days", "90 days", "182 days", "120 days"], 2, "FEMA treats a person as resident if he resided in India for more than 182 days during the preceding financial year, subject to conditions about the purpose of stay. Leaving India for employment or business can change this status."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "Under the Depositories Act, an investor subscribing to securities of an issuer has the option to:", ["Receive them in physical form or hold them through a depository", "Hold them only in the name of the depository participant chosen by the issuer", "Hold them only in physical form in all cases", "Hold them only with the registrar and transfer agent"], 0, "Section 8 of the Act gives the investor the option to hold securities either in physical form or with a depository. However, separate SEBI rules now mandate demat for certain issues and for transfers of listed securities."], ["SEBI (AIF) Regulations", "Under the AIF framework, which body issues the certificate that allows an investor to be treated as an 'accredited investor'?", ["The Income Tax Department", "An accreditation agency recognised by SEBI", "The branch of the bank where the investor holds a savings account", "The AIF manager itself"], 1, "Accreditation is granted by accreditation agencies, which are subsidiaries of depositories or stock exchanges recognised for this purpose, after verifying income or net worth. The fund manager cannot certify its own investors."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "Under the SEBI (FPI) Regulations, 2019, foreign portfolio investors are classified into how many categories?", ["Three", "Five", "Two", "Four"], 2, "The 2019 regulations simplified the earlier three-category system into two categories, Category I and Category II, based on the nature and regulation of the investor. Categorisation affects KYC and some investment conditions."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "Which of the following is 'generally available information' under the PIT Regulations?", ["Draft results shared with the statutory auditor for review", "Board discussions on a proposed acquisition that is not yet announced", "A merger proposal circulated among the deal team only", "Quarterly results disseminated through the stock exchange platform"], 3, "Generally available information is information accessible to the public on a non-discriminatory basis, such as results published through the exchange. Draft results and unannounced deal discussions are still UPSI."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "Which are the two depositories currently operating in India?", ["NSE and BSE", "NSCCL and ICCL", "CERSAI and CKYCR", "NSDL and CDSL"], 3, "National Securities Depository Ltd and Central Depository Services (India) Ltd are India's two depositories. NSE and BSE are stock exchanges, NSCCL and ICCL are clearing corporations, and CERSAI maintains CKYCR."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "In the books of the issuer, the depository is shown as the registered owner of demat securities. Which rights does the depository have in this capacity?", ["The right to receive dividends on the securities", "The right to sell the securities to recover fees", "Only the right to effect transfers, without voting or economic rights", "Full voting rights at all general meetings and the right to appoint directors"], 2, "Under the Act, the depository is deemed registered owner only for effecting transfer of ownership. It has no voting or other rights; all rights and benefits belong to the beneficial owner."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "In proceedings for action in case of default, which officer examines the matter and submits a report with recommendations to SEBI?", ["The designated authority", "The statutory auditor", "The intermediary's compliance officer", "The Securities Appellate Tribunal"], 0, "The Intermediaries Regulations provide for a designated authority to issue a show cause notice, consider replies and submit a report recommending action such as suspension or cancellation. SAT only hears appeals after an order is passed."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "What does the phrase 'tone at the top' mean in the context of compliance?", ["Top-performing sales staff being exempt from compliance checks", "Senior leadership visibly committing to ethical conduct and regulatory compliance", "Compliance officers being seated on the top floor of the head office building", "Regulators issuing directions only to large listed entities"], 1, "'Tone at the top' refers to the ethical climate set by the board and senior managers. When leaders prioritise compliance, staff follow; exempting top performers from checks would send the opposite message."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "Compliance risk is best described as the risk of:", ["Legal or regulatory sanctions, financial loss or reputational damage from not following rules", "Loss due to changes in the market price of securities held in a portfolio", "Loss caused by a sudden lack of buyers for an asset being sold", "Default by a borrower or counterparty on its contractual obligations"], 0, "Compliance risk arises from failing to comply with laws, regulations, codes and internal standards, leading to sanctions, financial loss or reputational harm. Market, credit and liquidity risk are separate risk types."], ["SEBI (InvIT) Regulations", "What is the main purpose of the requirement that the sponsor of an InvIT holds a minimum unitholding for a lock-in period after listing?", ["To exempt the InvIT from distribution requirements", "To prevent unitholders from ever selling their units", "To allow the sponsor to control all of the trustee's decisions on distributions", "To keep the sponsor's 'skin in the game' and align it with investors"], 3, "The sponsor's minimum holding and lock-in ensure that it remains committed to the assets it transferred, aligning its interests with unitholders. It does not restrict trading by other unitholders or override distribution norms."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "SEBI has cancelled the registration of a stock broker. What is the broker required to do in respect of its clients?", ["Transfer client securities to its promoters for safe custody", "Keep client money until the appeal period is over, without exception", "Continue trading for existing clients until their contracts expire", "Stop the activity and allow clients to withdraw or transfer their funds and securities"], 3, "On cancellation, the intermediary must cease the activity, allow clients to withdraw or transfer their securities and funds, and hand over records as directed. Continuing to trade or moving assets to promoters would be a violation."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "Under the Intermediaries Regulations, the 'fit and proper person' criteria apply to:", ["Only employees in the sales and marketing team", "Only the statutory auditor appointed by the intermediary", "The applicant and its principal officer, directors, promoters and key managerial persons", "Only the individual clients who open trading or demat accounts with the intermediary"], 2, "Fit and proper criteria apply to the applicant or intermediary as well as its principal officer, directors, promoters, persons in control and key managerial persons. Clients, auditors or sales staff are not the subject of these criteria."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "A KRA wants to use the KYC data in its system to sell targeted marketing lists to a third party. Under the KRA Regulations, this is:", ["Not permitted, as KRAs must maintain confidentiality of KYC data", "Permitted after informing the stock exchanges", "Permitted if the third party buying the data is a SEBI-registered intermediary", "Permitted if the data is more than five years old"], 0, "KRAs must keep KYC records confidential and use them only for the purposes permitted under the regulations. Selling client data for marketing breaches these obligations, regardless of the buyer."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "A broker is aggrieved by an order of the Securities Appellate Tribunal. Within how many days of communication of the order may it appeal to the Supreme Court?", ["30 days", "60 days", "45 days", "90 days"], 1, "Under Section 15Z, an appeal against a SAT decision lies to the Supreme Court within 60 days, extendable by up to another 60 days for sufficient cause. The 45-day limit applies to appeals from SEBI orders to SAT."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "An investor who is already KYC-compliant on a KRA opens an account with a new broker. What can the broker generally do?", ["Open the account without seeing any KYC record at all", "Refuse the account because KYC was done by another intermediary", "Insist on fresh in-person verification and a full set of new documents in all cases", "Fetch and rely on the KRA record, doing additional checks only where needed"], 3, "The KRA framework allows a new intermediary to download and rely on the existing KYC record, carrying out additional due diligence if its own risk policy requires. Repeating full KYC every time defeats the purpose of KRAs."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Once a reporting entity is satisfied that a transaction is suspicious, within what time must it file a Suspicious Transaction Report with FIU-IND?", ["24 hours", "7 working days", "30 days", "15 days"], 1, "Under the PML Rules, an STR must be furnished within seven working days of the Principal Officer arriving at a conclusion that the transaction is suspicious. Delay in reaching that conclusion should also be avoided."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "In the 'three lines of defence' model, which function is generally regarded as the third line?", ["Compliance and risk management", "Internal audit", "Business and operations teams", "External statutory auditors"], 1, "Business units are the first line, compliance and risk management form the second line, and internal audit provides independent assurance as the third line. Statutory auditors are external to the model."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "Which of the following is a money market instrument?", ["Ten-year government dated security", "Equity share of a listed company", "Unit of an open-ended equity mutual fund scheme", "Certificate of deposit issued by a bank"], 3, "Money market instruments have original maturities of up to one year and include treasury bills, commercial paper and certificates of deposit. A ten-year G-sec and equity shares are capital market instruments."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "An investor wants to pledge shares held in demat form as security for a loan. How must the pledge be created?", ["By signing a transfer deed in favour of the lender", "By handing over the delivery instruction slip book to the lender", "By informing the company's auditor in writing", "Through the depository system, with an entry recorded by the depository"], 3, "Section 12 of the Depositories Act requires a pledge or hypothecation of demat securities to be recorded with the depository on intimation through the participant. Handing over slips or signing a transfer deed does not create a valid pledge."], ["SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations", "During severe market stress, a debt scheme applies an adjustment that lowers the NAV for redeeming investors so that they bear part of the liquidity cost. This mechanism is called:", ["Exit load", "Swing pricing", "Side pocketing", "NAV smoothing"], 1, "Swing pricing adjusts NAV to pass on the cost of large redemptions to the investors causing them, protecting remaining unitholders. Side pocketing segregates a stressed security, and an exit load is a fixed charge on redemption."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "An insider wants to trade under a trading plan. Under the PIT Regulations, the trading plan must be:", ["Approved by the auditor and published in newspapers", "Registered with the depository and kept with the DP", "Approved by the compliance officer and disclosed to the stock exchanges", "Approved by SEBI and kept strictly confidential by the company until expiry"], 2, "A trading plan is presented to the compliance officer for approval and, once approved, is notified to the stock exchanges where the securities are listed. SEBI, the auditor and the DP do not approve trading plans."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "Under the Intermediaries Regulations, a certificate of registration once granted is generally:", ["Valid only until the intermediary changes its auditor", "Permanent, unless suspended or cancelled, subject to payment of fees", "Valid for five years and automatically lapses after that period ends", "Valid for one year and renewable every financial year"], 1, "The regulations provide for a permanent certificate of registration, subject to payment of applicable fees and continued compliance. It remains valid unless SEBI suspends or cancels it or the intermediary surrenders it."], ["SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations", "In a mutual fund structure, which entity holds the scheme's securities in safe custody?", ["The custodian", "The registrar and transfer agent", "The mutual fund distributor", "The sponsor"], 0, "A SEBI-registered custodian holds and safeguards the scheme's securities and handles related settlement and corporate actions. The RTA maintains investor records, and the sponsor sets up the fund but does not hold its assets."], ["SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations", "Since April 2021, the dividend option in mutual fund schemes is required to be named:", ["Guaranteed Income Payout Option (GIPO)", "Bonus Reinvestment Option (BRO)", "Systematic Profit Distribution (SPD)", "Income Distribution cum Capital Withdrawal (IDCW)"], 3, "SEBI required dividend options to be renamed IDCW so that investors understand that distributions may be paid partly out of their own capital. The other names do not exist in the regulations."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "SEBI was set up in 1988 as a non-statutory body. In which year did it get statutory powers through an Act of Parliament?", ["1996", "1992", "1988", "2002"], 1, "SEBI was constituted in 1988 by a government resolution and acquired statutory status under the SEBI Act, 1992. 1996 is the year of the Depositories Act, and 2002 saw major amendments to the SEBI Act."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "An FPI's holding in a listed company crosses the individual investment limit. What are its options under the regulations?", ["Keep the excess holding indefinitely if the investee company's board does not object", "Divest the excess within five trading days or have it reclassified as FDI", "Convert the excess shares into a bank fixed deposit", "Transfer the excess shares to a resident Indian relative"], 1, "If the limit is breached, the FPI may divest the excess holding within five trading days of the settlement of the trades or the investment is treated as FDI, subject to FDI conditions. Other options listed are not permitted."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "Trades in which the buyer and seller are the same person, so there is no real change in beneficial ownership, are known as:", ["Odd-lot trades", "Wash trades", "Spot trades", "Bulk deals"], 1, "Wash trades do not transfer beneficial ownership and only create a misleading appearance of trading. PFUTP prohibits any act that creates a false or misleading appearance of trading in the securities market."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "Since April 2019, SEBI requires transfers of securities of listed companies to be done:", ["In either form at the investor's choice without restriction", "Only in demat form, except transmission or transposition", "Only through the company's registered office", "Only in physical form with a signed transfer deed"], 1, "SEBI amended listing norms so that, from 1 April 2019, transfer of listed securities can be effected only in dematerialised form. Requests for transmission or transposition are an exception."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "A company is being wound up. In what order are the following generally paid out of its assets?", ["Preference and equity shareholders share equally", "Secured creditors first, equity shareholders last", "All claimants are paid in proportion to holdings", "Equity shareholders first, debenture holders last"], 1, "Debt holders have a contractual, prior claim, with secured creditors ranking ahead of unsecured ones. Equity shareholders are residual claimants and receive only what remains after all creditors and preference holders are paid."], ["SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations", "All schemes of a mutual fund together cannot own more than what percentage of the paid-up capital carrying voting rights of any single company?", ["15%", "5%", "10%", "25%"], 2, "The Seventh Schedule of the MF Regulations prohibits a mutual fund, across all its schemes, from owning more than 10% of any company's paid-up capital carrying voting rights. This prevents mutual funds from gaining control of companies."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "A fund manager wants to set up a fund in GIFT City, Gujarat. Which regulator will register and supervise this activity in the International Financial Services Centre?", ["RBI", "IFSCA", "SEBI", "Gujarat State Government"], 1, "The International Financial Services Centres Authority (IFSCA) is the unified regulator for financial services in IFSCs, including GIFT City. Fund management entities there register with IFSCA, not with SEBI."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "Which body, chaired by the Union Finance Minister, coordinates among financial sector regulators on financial stability matters?", ["Board for Financial Supervision of the Reserve Bank", "Securities Appellate Tribunal", "Monetary Policy Committee", "Financial Stability and Development Council"], 3, "The Financial Stability and Development Council (FSDC) is chaired by the Finance Minister and includes heads of the financial regulators. The Monetary Policy Committee sets the policy rate and is headed by the RBI Governor."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "Under the SCRA, which body may make or amend the bye-laws of a recognised stock exchange, subject to approval?", ["The recognised stock exchange itself, with SEBI's previous approval", "Any group of investors holding its shares, through a resolution passed at a meeting", "The listed companies through a joint resolution", "The Comptroller and Auditor General of India"], 0, "A recognised stock exchange may make bye-laws for regulating contracts, subject to SEBI's previous approval, and SEBI can also make or amend bye-laws itself. Investors, listed companies or the CAG cannot do so."], ["SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations", "Under the 'skin in the game' framework, what must AMCs do with part of the compensation of their key employees?", ["Pay it entirely in cash in advance every year", "Pay it into a fixed deposit with the sponsor's bank for three years", "Pay it in units of the schemes they manage, subject to a lock-in", "Pay it into the trustees' personal accounts"], 2, "SEBI requires a portion of the compensation of designated AMC employees to be paid in units of the schemes they oversee, locked in for a specified period. This aligns their interests with unitholders."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "A firm wants to start business as a portfolio manager. Under the SEBI Act, what must it do before commencing activity?", ["Obtain a certificate of registration from SEBI", "Register only with the Registrar of Companies", "Obtain a licence from the Reserve Bank of India", "Seek approval from a recognised stock exchange"], 0, "Section 12 of the SEBI Act prohibits any intermediary such as a portfolio manager, broker or investment adviser from dealing in securities without a certificate of registration from SEBI. ROC incorporation alone is not sufficient."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "Which online platform does SEBI use for receiving and tracking investor complaints against registered intermediaries?", ["MCA21", "CERSAI", "SCORES", "e-FRRO"], 2, "SCORES (SEBI Complaints Redress System) lets investors lodge complaints online, and intermediaries must respond within the timelines prescribed. MCA21 is the corporate affairs portal and CERSAI maintains CKYC and security interest records."], ["SEBI (InvIT) Regulations", "An InvIT wants its aggregate consolidated borrowings to exceed 25% of the value of its assets. Which conditions apply?", ["No conditions apply, as InvITs can borrow without any limit", "Obtain a credit rating and approval of unitholders", "Approval of only the project manager", "Prior approval of RBI for every borrowing"], 1, "Borrowings beyond 25% of asset value require a credit rating and approval of unitholders, and higher levels are subject to additional conditions such as a strong rating and distribution track record. Borrowing is not unlimited."], ["Foreign Exchange Management Act", "Under the Liberalised Remittance Scheme, a resident individual can currently remit up to what amount per financial year for permitted transactions?", ["US$ 250,000", "US$ 100,000", "US$ 1,000,000", "US$ 500,000"], 0, "The LRS allows resident individuals to remit up to US$ 250,000 per financial year for permitted current and capital account transactions, such as education, travel and overseas investment."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "Who among the following CANNOT register as a foreign portfolio investor?", ["A non-resident Indian investing in his individual capacity", "A regulated foreign mutual fund", "A foreign pension fund that is appropriately regulated in its home country", "A foreign central bank"], 0, "NRIs and OCIs investing as individuals have their own investment routes and cannot register as FPIs, though they may be constituents of an FPI within limits. Foreign pension funds, central banks and regulated funds can register."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "The board of a listed company must ensure maintenance of which record of persons with whom UPSI is shared?", ["A newspaper notice listing all such recipients", "A physical register signed only by the Chairman", "A list of names handed over to the statutory auditor once every financial year", "A structured digital database with names and PAN or other identifiers"], 3, "The PIT Regulations require a structured digital database containing the nature of UPSI shared and names and PAN (or other identifiers) of persons with whom it is shared, with adequate internal controls such as time-stamping and audit trails."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "To whom do the prohibitions under the PFUTP Regulations apply?", ["Only listed companies, their promoters and the directors on their boards", "Only foreign portfolio investors", "Only stock brokers registered with SEBI", "Any person dealing in securities, whether or not a SEBI intermediary"], 3, "PFUTP prohibitions are framed as 'no person shall', so they cover any person dealing in securities, including investors, companies and intermediaries. Restricting them to brokers or listed companies is incorrect."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "A Designated Depository Participant (DDP) under the FPI framework is generally:", ["A custodian or bank that is a depository participant approved by SEBI", "A credit rating agency registered with SEBI", "A stock exchange or clearing house located in the investor's home country", "Any individual chartered accountant in India"], 0, "DDPs are SEBI-approved entities, typically custodians that are also authorised dealer banks and depository participants. They grant FPI registration on SEBI's behalf and carry out KYC and monitoring."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "The Companies Act, 2013 is administered primarily by:", ["Securities Appellate Tribunal", "Ministry of Corporate Affairs", "Department of Economic Affairs", "Reserve Bank of India"], 1, "The Ministry of Corporate Affairs administers the Companies Act, 2013, along with bodies such as the Registrar of Companies. SEBI administers securities-specific provisions for listed companies, but company law as a whole sits with MCA."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "A person posts fabricated 'insider tips' about a small company on social media and sells his holdings after the price jumps. This is best described as:", ["An offer for sale through the stock exchange", "A permitted expression of personal investment opinion", "A buy-back of shares by the company", "A 'pump and dump' scheme using false information"], 3, "Spreading false or misleading information to inflate a price and then selling is a fraudulent and manipulative practice under PFUTP. Genuine, honest opinion is different from knowingly circulating false tips."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "Under the SCRA, a 'spot delivery contract' is one that provides for:", ["Delivery on a fixed date one year after the trade date", "Delivery and payment on the same day or the next day", "Delivery within one month of the trade date", "Settlement by paying only the price difference"], 1, "Spot delivery contracts involve actual delivery of securities and payment of price on the same day or the next day. Contracts settled only by price differences or for future dates are forward or derivative contracts."], ["Foreign Exchange Management Act", "Where the amount involved in a FEMA contravention is quantifiable, the maximum penalty is up to:", ["Twice the sum involved", "₹2 lakh irrespective of the amount", "Three times the sum involved", "The sum involved plus interest"], 2, "Section 13 of FEMA provides a penalty of up to three times the sum involved where quantifiable, or up to ₹2 lakh where it is not, plus a further daily penalty for continuing contraventions."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "Who is generally responsible within an intermediary for monitoring adherence to securities laws and for redressal of investor grievances?", ["The SEBI regional office in that city", "The statutory auditor appointed by the intermediary's shareholders", "The largest client of the intermediary", "The compliance officer appointed by the intermediary"], 3, "Intermediaries must appoint a compliance officer who monitors compliance with the Act, rules, regulations and circulars and handles investor grievances. The auditor reviews accounts, and SEBI's regional office is the regulator, not an internal officer."], ["SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations", "A mutual fund scheme wants to borrow money. Under the MF Regulations, it may borrow only:", ["To meet temporary liquidity needs for redemptions or dividend payouts, within limits", "To fund the AMC's office expenses", "To take leveraged positions to boost returns", "To lend money at attractive interest rates to the sponsor's group companies"], 0, "Schemes may borrow only to meet temporary liquidity needs for repurchase, redemption or payout, capped at 20% of net assets and for a maximum of six months. Borrowing for leverage or for the AMC or sponsor is not allowed."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "A recognised stock exchange is granted recognition under the SCRA. In practice, this power is exercised by:", ["The exchange's own board of directors", "The Reserve Bank of India for all exchanges", "SEBI, to which the Central Government has delegated the power", "The Ministry of Corporate Affairs through the Registrar of Companies"], 2, "Recognition of stock exchanges under Section 4 is a Central Government power that has been delegated to SEBI. SEBI therefore grants, renews and can withdraw recognition of stock exchanges."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "Which of the following is a duty of an intermediary under the KRA Regulations?", ["Prescribe the KYC forms and formats to be used across the entire securities market", "Approve the registration of other KRAs", "Carry out initial KYC and in-person verification before uploading", "Audit the KRA's systems every month"], 2, "The intermediary performs initial KYC, including in-person verification where required, and uploads the record. SEBI registers KRAs and prescribes KYC formats, while system audits of KRAs are not the intermediary's job."], ["SEBI (REIT) Regulations", "Under the REIT Regulations, a valuer cannot value the same property for more than how many consecutive years?", ["Ten years", "Six years", "Four years", "Two years"], 2, "To preserve independence, a valuer may not undertake valuation of the same property for more than four consecutive years, and can be reappointed only after a gap. The valuer also must not be an associate of the sponsor, manager or trustee."], ["SEBI (AIF) Regulations", "Which of the following statements about the structure of AIFs is correct?", ["Category I AIFs may be open-ended with daily NAV, but Category III AIFs must always be close-ended", "Only Category II AIFs may be close-ended", "All AIFs must be open-ended with daily NAV", "Category I and II AIFs must be close-ended; Category III may be open-ended or close-ended"], 3, "Category I and II AIFs must be close-ended with a minimum tenure of three years. Category III AIFs, which use trading strategies, can be either open-ended or close-ended."], ["SEBI (AIF) Regulations", "A Category II AIF wants to invest in a company that is an associate of its manager. Under the AIF Regulations, such investment generally requires:", ["Approval of the stock exchange where the units of the AIF scheme are listed", "Approval only of the manager's board of directors", "Approval of at least 75% of the investors by value of their investment", "No approval, since associates are always permitted"], 2, "Category I and II AIFs may invest in associates only with the approval of 75% of investors by value of their investment in the scheme, to address conflicts of interest. Manager approval alone is not sufficient."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "Which statement about interoperability among KRAs is correct?", ["An intermediary can access a client's KYC record through any KRA", "A client must complete KYC with each KRA separately", "Each intermediary must register with every KRA separately for each client", "Only the KRA that first stored the record can ever view it"], 0, "KRAs are required to be interoperable, so a KYC record uploaded to one KRA can be accessed by intermediaries connected to another KRA. The client does not need separate KYC for each KRA."], ["Foreign Exchange Management Act", "A foreign company invests in an Indian company in a sector open to 100% FDI under the automatic route. What approval is needed before the investment?", ["Prior approval of SEBI's board", "Prior approval of the Union Cabinet in every case, irrespective of the amount invested", "Prior approval of the State Government", "No prior government approval; only post-investment reporting is required"], 3, "Under the automatic route, no prior approval of the Government or RBI is needed; the investee company must report the investment within the prescribed timelines. The approval route applies only to specified sectors or limits."], ["SEBI (AIF) Regulations", "Under the AIF Regulations, the private placement memorandum of an AIF scheme must be filed with SEBI through:", ["The fund's statutory auditor", "A stock broker registered with an exchange", "A SEBI-registered merchant banker", "A credit rating agency"], 2, "SEBI requires the PPM of AIF schemes to be filed through a merchant banker, who carries out due diligence on the disclosures. Brokers, auditors and rating agencies do not perform this role."], ["Foreign Exchange Management Act", "A resident realises he has made an inadvertent technical contravention of FEMA. Which mechanism lets him settle it by paying a sum instead of facing adjudication?", ["Compounding of the contravention", "Applying under the SEBI settlement framework", "Registering as a foreign portfolio investor", "Filing a Suspicious Transaction Report"], 0, "FEMA contraventions can be compounded by RBI or the Directorate of Enforcement on payment of a compounding amount, closing the matter without adjudication. The SEBI settlement framework applies to securities-law proceedings."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "Which of the following is a preventive compliance control rather than a detective one?", ["Training employees on new SEBI circulars before they take effect", "Reviewing trade logs after month-end to find exceptions", "Investigating a complaint received through SCORES", "Conducting an internal audit of all of last year's completed transactions"], 0, "Preventive controls stop breaches before they occur, such as training, approvals and system checks. Post-facto reviews, audits and complaint investigations detect problems that have already happened."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "A single FPI, together with its investor group, must hold less than what share of the total paid-up equity capital of an Indian company on a fully diluted basis?", ["49%", "10%", "24%", "5%"], 1, "Investment by a single FPI including its investor group must be below 10% of the company's total paid-up equity capital on a fully diluted basis. If this is breached, the FPI must divest within the permitted time or the holding is reclassified as FDI."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "A bank accepts one-year fixed deposits from households and uses them to give five-year term loans to small businesses. This is best described as:", ["Maturity transformation by a financial intermediary", "Direct finance raised through the capital market by issuers", "Price discovery in the secondary market", "Disinvestment of public sector holdings"], 0, "The bank converts short-term deposits into longer-term loans, which is maturity transformation, a key service of intermediaries. Direct finance would involve the business raising money from investors without the bank in between."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "Trading in commodity futures on a national commodity derivatives exchange is currently regulated by:", ["RBI", "Forward Markets Commission", "Ministry of Consumer Affairs", "SEBI"], 3, "The Forward Markets Commission was merged with SEBI in 2015, and since then SEBI regulates commodity derivatives exchanges and brokers. The FMC no longer exists as a separate regulator."], ["SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations", "Within how many working days of a valid redemption request must an AMC generally transfer redemption proceeds to the investor?", ["Fifteen working days", "Ten working days", "Seven working days", "Three working days"], 3, "SEBI has shortened the redemption timeline so that proceeds must generally be paid within three working days, with limited exceptions for schemes investing significantly overseas or in other specified situations."], ["SEBI (REIT) Regulations", "Under the framework introduced in 2024, a Small and Medium REIT (SM REIT) is meant for assets of value in the range of:", ["₹5 crore to ₹25 crore", "₹50 crore to less than ₹500 crore", "Above ₹5,000 crore only", "₹500 crore to below ₹1,000 crore"], 1, "SEBI created SM REITs in 2024 for asset sizes from ₹50 crore up to less than ₹500 crore, bringing fractional ownership platforms under regulation. Larger portfolios continue under the main REIT framework."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "At what stage must an intermediary satisfy the 'fit and proper person' criteria?", ["Only at the time of applying for initial registration", "At the time of registration and on a continuous basis thereafter", "Only when SEBI conducts a periodic inspection of the intermediary", "Only when the intermediary renews its office lease"], 1, "Being fit and proper is a continuing requirement, not a one-time check. If a person later ceases to meet the criteria, SEBI can take action against the intermediary."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "Penalty amounts realised by SEBI under the SEBI Act are credited to:", ["The investor's demat account directly", "SEBI's own General Fund for salaries", "The Consolidated Fund of India", "The Settlement Guarantee Fund of the exchange"], 2, "Section 15JA requires sums realised by way of penalties to be credited to the Consolidated Fund of India. Disgorged amounts are handled separately, for example through the Investor Protection and Education Fund."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "Which regulator supervises insurance companies operating in India?", ["IRDAI", "PFRDA", "NABARD", "SEBI"], 0, "The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India (IRDAI) regulates insurers and insurance intermediaries. PFRDA regulates pension funds and the NPS, and NABARD is a development finance institution for agriculture and rural areas."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "A compliance officer finds that a scheme breached an investment limit. The business head asks her not to record it because the position has since been corrected. What should she do?", ["Delete the record since the position is now within the limit", "Wait until the next audit to see if the auditor notices it", "Ask the fund manager to sign an undertaking and then quietly close the matter without any report", "Document the breach and escalate it as per policy and regulatory reporting requirements"], 3, "Even corrected breaches must be recorded, reported to the board or trustees and to the regulator where required. Hiding a breach is itself a serious compliance failure and damages the integrity of the function."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "A company raises fresh capital by offering its shares to the public for the first time through an IPO. In which market does this transaction take place?", ["Over-the-counter derivatives market", "Secondary market", "Primary market", "Money market"], 2, "An IPO is a fresh issue of securities by the company itself, so the money flows to the issuer. That is the primary market. The secondary market only involves trading of already-issued securities between investors."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "Which of the following markets is primarily regulated by RBI rather than SEBI?", ["Units of mutual fund schemes", "Listed equity shares on stock exchanges", "Government securities and money market", "Units of REITs and InvITs"], 2, "RBI regulates the government securities, money and foreign exchange markets. Listed equities, mutual funds, REITs and InvITs all fall under SEBI's jurisdiction as part of the securities market."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "The Central KYC Records Registry (CKYCR) assigns each customer a KYC Identification Number (KIN) of how many digits?", ["16", "14", "12", "10"], 1, "CKYCR, maintained by CERSAI, issues a 14-digit KYC Identification Number that the customer can quote to other regulated entities. A 10-character code is a PAN and a 12-digit number is Aadhaar."], ["SEBI (REIT) Regulations", "For a retail investor, which is a key benefit of investing in a listed REIT?", ["Freedom from all market and interest rate risks", "Direct ownership of a specific office floor in the building that the investor can occupy personally", "A guaranteed fixed return set by SEBI each year", "Exposure to income-generating commercial real estate with small amounts and exchange liquidity"], 3, "REITs let investors own a share of a diversified portfolio of rent-generating properties with small ticket sizes and the ability to trade on exchanges. Returns are not guaranteed, and unitholders do not own specific floors."], ["SEBI (REIT) Regulations", "Units of a REIT that are offered to the public must be:", ["Held by only one institutional investor", "Issued only in physical certificate form", "Redeemable daily at NAV by the manager", "Listed on a recognised stock exchange"], 3, "Publicly offered REIT units are mandatorily listed on a recognised stock exchange, which provides trading liquidity. REIT units are not redeemed daily like open-ended mutual fund units and are held in demat form."], ["Foreign Exchange Management Act", "Which of the following is a current account transaction under FEMA?", ["A long-term loan raised from a foreign lender", "Payment for imported goods in the ordinary course of business", "Purchase of a residential flat abroad by a resident", "Acquisition of shares of a foreign company by a resident individual"], 1, "Current account transactions do not alter assets or liabilities outside India; payments for imports, travel and services are typical examples. Buying foreign property or shares and raising foreign loans are capital account transactions."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Under the PML Rules, for a client that is a company, a natural person is a beneficial owner if he has a controlling ownership interest of more than:", ["10% of shares, capital or profits", "5% of shares, capital or profits", "51% of shares, capital or profits", "25% of shares, capital or profits"], 0, "Following the 2023 amendment to the PML Rules, the threshold for a company is ownership or entitlement of more than 10% of shares, capital or profits, or control through other means. The earlier threshold was 25%."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "After completing the KYC of a new client, who is responsible for uploading the KYC details to the KRA system?", ["The client himself through the stock exchange", "The depository with which the client later opens a demat account", "The SEBI-registered intermediary that performed the KYC", "SEBI's regional office in the client's state"], 2, "The intermediary that performs initial KYC, including verification, must upload the details and documents to the KRA within the prescribed time. The client and SEBI do not upload KYC records themselves."], ["SEBI (AIF) Regulations", "A private equity fund that does not use leverage except for day-to-day operational needs and does not receive government incentives is usually registered as a:", ["Category I AIF", "Category II AIF", "Venture capital undertaking", "Category III AIF"], 1, "Category II covers AIFs such as private equity and debt funds that are not in Category I or III and do not use leverage other than for operational requirements. Category I is for funds with positive spillovers, and Category III for complex trading strategies."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "To be eligible for FPI registration, an applicant should generally be a resident of a country whose securities market regulator:", ["Has been in existence for at least 50 years", "Is a member of the Indian Banks' Association and has an office in India", "Has a permanent office located in Mumbai", "Is a signatory to the IOSCO MMoU or has a bilateral MoU with SEBI"], 3, "An FPI applicant must be from a jurisdiction whose securities regulator is a signatory to the IOSCO Multilateral MoU or has a bilateral MoU with SEBI, and it must not be from a jurisdiction identified by FATF as high-risk."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "A wealthy client who is a serving senior government official abroad wants to open a mutual fund folio. Under SEBI's AML guidelines, the intermediary should:", ["Apply enhanced due diligence and obtain senior management approval", "Refuse to open the account automatically, whatever the source of funds or documents", "Open the account immediately without additional checks", "Accept the account only if the investment is in cash"], 0, "Politically exposed persons are high-risk clients; intermediaries must apply enhanced due diligence, verify source of funds and obtain senior management approval. An automatic refusal is not required."], ["SEBI (AIF) Regulations", "A Category II AIF scheme has a corpus of ₹300 crore. What is the minimum continuing interest the manager or sponsor must hold in the scheme?", ["₹5 crore", "₹15 crore", "₹10 crore", "₹7.5 crore"], 0, "For Category I and II AIFs, the continuing interest is 2.5% of the corpus or ₹5 crore, whichever is lower. Here 2.5% of ₹300 crore is ₹7.5 crore, so the lower figure of ₹5 crore applies."]]};
